#align(center)[#text(size: 16pt, weight: "bold")[Abundance in numerical dimension one \
for terminal threefolds in positive characteristic]]
#align(center)[OpenAI]
#align(center)[September 24, 2026]

== 1 Introduction <sec:introduction>

The abundance conjecture predicts that a nef canonical divisor on a projective variety with mild singularities is semiample: some positive Cartier multiple is generated by its global sections. Such sections turn numerical information about the canonical divisor into a morphism that records the canonical geometry of the variety. For a nef divisor $L$ on a threefold, numerical dimension one means $L dot.op H^2 > 0$ and $L^2 dot.op H = 0$ for an ample Cartier divisor $H$. We prove this case of abundance for terminal threefolds in characteristic greater than three.

#thm([Theorem 1.1.])[
  #emph[Let $X$ be a normal projective $bb(Q)$-factorial threefold over an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p > 3$. Suppose that $X$ has terminal singularities and that $K_X$ is nef with $nu \( K_X \) = 1$. Then some positive Cartier multiple of $K_X$ has at least two independent global sections. In particular, $kappa \( X \, K_X \) = 1$ and $K_X$ is semiample.]
] <thm:main>

No assumption is made on the first Betti number, irregularity, or Albanese variety. Thus Theorem 1 resolves the numerical-dimension-one case of the abundance conjecture under the stated terminal and characteristic hypotheses.

Geometrically, the theorem gives a morphism $f : X arrow.r C$ with connected fibers to a normal projective curve and an ample Cartier divisor $A$ on $C$ such that $m K_X tilde.op f^(*) A$ for some $m > 0$. Indeed, the image of a morphism defined by a globally generated pluricanonical multiple is a curve, since $nu \( K_X \) = 1$. Its Stein factorization gives $f$; the finite pullback to $C$ of a hyperplane divisor on the image gives $A$.

=== 1.1 Context <context>

Over the complex numbers, Miyaoka proved the numerical-dimension-one case of threefold abundance by studying effective pluricanonical divisors, primitive connected cycles, and their infinitesimal neighborhoods #cite(label("miyaoka1988")). Kawamata’s abundance theorem for minimal threefolds includes another proof of this case using the log minimal model program and formal thickenings #cite(label("kawamata1992")) Section 4. The present argument develops this canonical-cycle framework in positive characteristic. More recently, Liu–Xu developed higher-dimensional numerical-dimension-one abundance over $bb(C)$ under nonvanishing hypotheses #cite(label("liuxu2025")). The ground field in the present theorem requires the positive-characteristic inputs described next.

The positive-characteristic minimal model program supplies the birational operations needed to study abundance for threefolds. Over algebraically closed fields of characteristic greater than five, Hacon–Xu established standard-coefficient flips and minimal models for projective $bb(Q)$-factorial terminal threefolds with pseudoeffective canonical divisor #cite(label("haconxu2015")) Theorems 1.1 and 1.2. Birkar proved flips for $bb(Q)$-factorial dlt threefold pairs and log minimal models for projective klt threefold pairs with pseudoeffective log canonical divisor #cite(label("birkar2016")) Theorems 1.1 and 1.2. The relative log minimal model program in characteristic greater than five was established by Hashizume, Nakamura and Tanaka #cite(label("hnt2020")); Hacon and Witaszek treated the characteristic-five case #cite(label("hw2022")).

In characteristic greater than five, Xu–Zhang proved nonvanishing for terminal minimal threefolds #cite(label("xuzhang2019")); Witaszek obtained nonvanishing for klt threefold pairs and abundance in nef dimension at most two through a canonical bundle formula #cite(label("witaszek2021")). Zhang proved abundance for minimal threefolds with nontrivial Albanese map #cite(label("zhang2020")). Xu proved nonvanishing for pseudoeffective log canonical threefold pairs in characteristic greater than three and semiampleness when the nef log canonical divisor has positive Kodaira dimension #cite(label("xu2024")) Theorems 1.4 and 1.6. His theorem for nef dimension at most two #cite(label("xu2024")) Theorem 1.8 and his subsequent numerical-dimension-two abundance theorem #cite(label("xu2026")) Theorem 1.1 leave a specific issue in the present setting: an effective canonical divisor of numerical dimension one could a priori still have Kodaira dimension zero and maximal nef dimension. Theorem 1 excludes that possibility. Here nef dimension is the dimension of the nef reduction, and maximal nef dimension means that no curve of canonical degree zero passes through a very general point. It is a different invariant from numerical dimension. The new step is the production of a second pluricanonical section; the existence of a first section and the passage from positive Kodaira dimension to semiampleness are Xu’s results.

Our tangent-sheaf argument follows the positive-characteristic foliation method appearing in Xu–Zhang #cite(label("xuzhang2019")) Section 2. It uses the Bogomolov inequality for strongly semistable sheaves with respect to a nef tuple, in Langer’s formulation #cite(label("langer2004")) Theorem 3.2; the corrections in #cite(label("langeradd2004")) preserve that theorem. Surface abundance enters through abundance for projective semi-log-canonical surfaces, proved by Tanaka over algebraically closed fields #cite(label("tanaka2016")) Theorem 0.1 and by Posva over arbitrary fields #cite(label("posva2024")) Theorem 3.1.

The descent mechanism is classical Cartier theory. In particular, Katz’s proof of Cartier descent supplies the horizontal projector and truncated Taylor expansion #cite(label("katz1970")) Theorem 5.1. We establish the completed-ring and bounded-pole statements that allow this algebra to be used on the punctured formal neighborhood of a nonreduced cycle. The three-row jet calculation and its uniform pole control are the bridge between the canonical-cycle geometry and this descent argument.

=== 1.2 The argument <the-argument>

After nonvanishing and the birational preparation in Section 2, the hypothetical maximal-nef-dimension case produces a smooth projective threefold $V$ and a connected effective nef Cartier cycle $D$ with primitive multiplicities. The cycle is numerically trivial on each of its components, its normal line $cal(O)_D \( D \)$ is torsion, and, on a neighborhood of $D$, a $p$-power of $omega_V$ has a divisor supported on $D$. The preparation proves torsion on the full nonreduced cycle, which is needed in the subsequent calculation.

Section 3 then proves strong semistability of the tangent and cotangent bundles for the nef degree $c_1 \( - \) dot.op H dot.op D$. The foliation quotient of a destabilizing sheaf would produce $D$-trivial curves through very general points. The resulting semistability gives $c_2 \( V \) dot.op D gt.eq 0$ and uniform bounds on polar parts, which are the inputs to the jet calculation.

The central calculation in Section 4 concerns finite jets along $D$, that is, sections on successive Cartier thickenings. It keeps all three cohomological rows of the pole filtration and compares their ranks over the periods $r p^e$, where $r$ is the order of $cal(O)_D \( D \)$. The scalar rows identify the completed canonical line with the line of an integral divisor supported on $D$, and force both $c_2 \( V \) dot.op D = 0$ and the precise limiting ratio $p \/ \( p + 1 \)$ for the last trivialization widths. These facts control the positions and lengths of differentials for vector bundles as well.

Section 5 turns the calculation into a formal-neighborhood criterion, Theorem 20: a bundle satisfying an Euler identity, bounded polar parts, and a concentration condition for leading jets has vanishing first Laurent cohomology. A bound on the source cuts of incoming differentials ensures that the constructed primitives actually converge with bounded poles. We prove this criterion directly for finite covers and completed lattices. The criterion applies to one-forms and exact forms.

Section 6 uses these vanishings to make every line bundle on the punctured formal neighborhood a $p$th power, by Cartier descent. On the other hand, intersection with $H$ on the components of $D$ defines a nonzero integer-valued homomorphism on its Picard group. Such a homomorphism cannot exist on a $p$-divisible group. The contradiction proves Theorem 1.

Figure 1 records the two conclusions about the same Picard group that give the contradiction. In the figure, $cal(R) = hat(cal(O))_V \( * D \)$ denotes functions on the completion along $D$ with a finite pole bound; its Picard group consists of locally free rank-one $cal(R)$-modules on $\| D \|$.

